================================================================================
NAME
       propagation - resonance-time flow across triads, patterns, and multi-
                     agent constellations in RTT

SYNOPSIS
       Propagation governs:
           • resonance flow
           • structural transitions
           • pattern evolution
           • multi-agent synchronization
           • lineage continuity

DESCRIPTION
       Propagation is the process by which resonance-time flows through triads,
       across patterns, and between agents in a constellation. It determines
       how structural effects move, accumulate, dissipate, or transform across
       cycles.

       Propagation is not movement in physical time. It is structural flow in
       resonance-time (RT-space), where triads act as discrete sampling points
       and patterns act as flow geometries.

       Propagation ensures:
           • coherence across cycles
           • predictable pattern behavior
           • stable multi-agent coordination
           • schema-aligned transitions
           • lineage integrity

PROPAGATION THROUGH TRIADS
       Each triad is a resonance-time conduit:

           P1 (begin)
               → resonance alignment
               → baseline state captured

           P2 (transform)
               → resonance shift applied
               → operator signatures executed

           P3 (close)
               → resonance integrated
               → lineage updated

       Propagation across triads is the fundamental unit of RT flow.

       Triads propagate:
           • amplitude
           • polarity
           • gradient
           • curvature
           • structural metadata

PROPAGATION THROUGH PATTERNS
       Patterns define the geometry of resonance flow.

       sequential
              Linear propagation with minimal distortion.

       nested
              Recursive propagation; resonance folds inward.

       spiral
              Resonance deepens with each cycle; gradient amplifies.

       ladder
              Resonance ascends in discrete steps; coherence increases.

       expansion
              Resonance branches outward; multi-path propagation.

       mirror
              Resonance direction inverts; polarity flips.

       weave
              Resonance interleaves across concurrent threads.

       constellation
              Resonance distributes across multiple agents.

       cascade
              Resonance flows through dependent stages.

       Patterns determine:
           • propagation speed
           • propagation direction
           • propagation stability
           • propagation complexity

PROPAGATION IN MULTI-AGENT CONSTELLATIONS
       Constellations are multi-agent resonance networks.

       Each agent:
           • maintains its own triads
           • propagates resonance locally
           • exchanges resonance metadata with neighbors

       Constellation propagation requires:
           • alignment of resonance signatures
           • compatible schemas
           • synchronized lineage
           • stable coherence thresholds

       Constellation propagation modes:

           broadcast
                  One agent propagates resonance to all others.

           ripple
                  Resonance spreads outward in waves.

           mesh
                  Resonance flows through a network of agents.

           harmonic-sync
                  Agents align resonance phases.

           divergence
                  Agents propagate resonance in different directions.

       WRSADC ensures safe propagation across agents.

PROPAGATION VARIABLES
       Propagation depends on:

           R   resonance amplitude
           G   resonance gradient
           P   polarity
           C   coherence
           L   lineage continuity
           S   schema compliance

       Propagation is stable when:
           • gradients remain within thresholds
           • polarity does not oscillate uncontrollably
           • lineage remains continuous
           • schemas remain satisfied

PROPAGATION FAILURE MODES
       drift
              Resonance slowly deviates from expected trajectory.

       fragmentation
              Propagation splits into incoherent branches.

       inversion-loop
              Polarity flips repeatedly; mirror recursion.

       overload
              Resonance amplitude exceeds safe thresholds.

       desync
              Multi-agent propagation loses phase alignment.

       WRSADC detects and prevents all failure modes.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       Schemas define:
           • allowed propagation paths
           • resonance boundaries
           • pattern compatibility
           • multi-agent constraints

       Propagation must satisfy:
           • resonance-schema
           • structural-schema
           • lineage-schema
           • pattern-schema

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • resonance snapshots
           • propagation history
           • gradient evolution
           • polarity shifts
           • pattern transitions

       Propagation depends on lineage continuity.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • validates propagation safety
           • computes gradients and polarity
           • selects or switches patterns
           • applies overlays when needed
           • blocks unsafe propagation

       WRSADC is the propagation gatekeeper.

INTERACTION WITH THE ENGINE
       The Engine:
           • executes resonance transformations
           • updates internal RT-state
           • integrates propagation effects

       The Engine assumes propagation is safe; WRSADC guarantees it.

NOTES
       Propagation is the lifeblood of RTT. It is how resonance moves, how
       patterns evolve, and how multi-agent systems remain coherent across
       cycles. Without stable propagation, RTT collapses into noise.

